1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,048
Add a Schedule Trigger and a Google Sheets node exactly as you did in Section 5.

2
00:00:05,048 --> 00:00:09,923
There is a real lesson hiding in how little there is to explain here: once you have

3
00:00:09,923 --> 00:00:14,562
built one agent properly, the next one starts from a head start, not from zero.

4
00:00:14,562 --> 00:00:19,261
There is even a legitimate shortcut, and it is worth doing once to learn that it

5
00:00:19,261 --> 00:00:24,135
exists: duplicate the Agent 3 workflow from the workflow list, rename the copy, and

6
00:00:24,135 --> 00:00:28,129
strip out the report prompt, keeping the trigger and the sheet read.

7
00:00:28,129 --> 00:00:32,475
Two working nodes, inherited in ten seconds, credentials already attached.

8
00:00:32,475 --> 00:00:35,705
One discipline comes with the move: read what you kept.

9
00:00:35,705 --> 00:00:40,521
An inherited node carries every setting of its old job, and the read only dropdown

10
00:00:40,521 --> 00:00:45,219
check from Section 5.2 applies to a duplicated node exactly as it did to a fresh

11
00:00:45,219 --> 00:00:45,454
one.

12
00:00:45,454 --> 00:00:49,213
Copying a workflow copies its virtues and its assumptions alike.

13
00:00:49,763 --> 00:00:54,068
This is worth pausing on: the "three weeks" threshold is a judgement call you are

14
00:00:54,068 --> 00:00:56,194
encoding, not a fact the data hands you.

15
00:00:56,194 --> 00:01:00,553
Write down your own number before you build this, the same way you would decide it

16
00:01:00,553 --> 00:01:02,998
for a person you were training to do this job.

17
00:01:02,998 --> 00:01:05,443
Notice how this number sits against Agent 3's.

18
00:01:05,443 --> 00:01:09,695
The Monday report worries at fourteen days; the chaser starts drafting at twenty

19
00:01:09,695 --> 00:01:09,908
one.

20
00:01:09,908 --> 00:01:14,372
That ordering is the system working: the chase goes out while there is still runway,

21
00:01:14,372 --> 00:01:18,678
so that by the time a renewal enters the report's worry window, it should already

22
00:01:18,678 --> 00:01:20,272
have a reply thread behind it.

23
00:01:20,272 --> 00:01:23,355
Two different numbers, two different jobs, chosen to mesh.

24
00:01:23,355 --> 00:01:27,661
When you set your own thresholds, set them as a pair on purpose: the nudge should

25
00:01:27,661 --> 00:01:29,202
always fire before the alarm.

26
00:01:29,752 --> 00:01:33,021
This is a small detail with a real cost if you skip it.

27
00:01:33,021 --> 00:01:37,955
A stale "days remaining" number, typed once and never updated, will eventually tell

28
00:01:37,955 --> 00:01:41,224
your agent a renewal is safely far away when it is not.

29
00:01:41,224 --> 00:01:46,217
The mechanics stay code free: n8n's date handling can subtract the renewal date from

30
00:01:46,217 --> 00:01:51,210
today inside a small transformation step, one setting, no formula language to learn,

31
00:01:51,210 --> 00:01:56,085
and the course template does it exactly that way, so import C04 later and read how

32
00:01:56,085 --> 00:01:58,046
if you prefer learning backwards.

33
00:01:58,046 --> 00:02:03,039
The principle underneath is the one to keep: anything that changes with the calendar

34
00:02:03,039 --> 00:02:05,595
must be computed at run time, never stored.

35
00:02:05,595 --> 00:02:10,291
A stored "days left" is a snapshot pretending to be a fact, which is becoming a

36
00:02:10,291 --> 00:02:12,193
familiar villain in this course.

37
00:02:12,743 --> 00:02:17,209
The selection itself is one more node, a Filter, sitting between the sheet and the

38
00:02:17,209 --> 00:02:21,730
agent, with two plain conditions: Status is Open, and days remaining is your number

39
00:02:21,730 --> 00:02:22,220
or fewer.

40
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:26,032
What does this buy over just asking the model to pick the rows itself?

41
00:02:26,032 --> 00:02:30,607
The filter is arithmetic, so it is either right or wrong, checkable in one glance at

42
00:02:30,607 --> 00:02:31,697
what passed through.

43
00:02:31,697 --> 00:02:35,781
Save the model for the part that needs a model, the wording, and let a dumb

44
00:02:35,781 --> 00:02:37,633
comparison decide who gets chased.

45
00:02:37,633 --> 00:02:42,208
One box selects, one box writes, and when a chase goes to the wrong client, you will

46
00:02:42,208 --> 00:02:46,238
know within a minute which of the two to open, because they cannot both be

47
00:02:46,238 --> 00:02:46,892
responsible.

48
00:02:47,442 --> 00:02:52,287
Check the pinned rows against the filter by hand once: with the course's five-client

49
00:02:52,287 --> 00:02:57,075
pipeline, one open renewal sits inside a twenty one day window and should pass, the

50
00:02:57,075 --> 00:03:01,805
closed one must never pass whatever its date says, and the far-off ones wait their

51
00:03:01,805 --> 00:03:02,093
turn.

52
00:03:02,093 --> 00:03:06,881
If your filter passes a different set than your head does, one of you is wrong, and

53
00:03:06,881 --> 00:03:09,188
it is cheaper to find out which one now.

54
00:03:09,188 --> 00:03:13,687
Next lecture is where those close-to-deadline rows turn into an actual drafted

55
00:03:13,687 --> 00:03:14,956
email, one per client.
